<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shapes on Our Plates – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 10px;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes on Our Plates</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Square Cracker.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Name primary and secondary colors. Interactive: Answer questions using simple words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify and name primary and secondary colors when describing the color and shape of food items (e.g., "This is a red apple." "The brown cracker is square."). Can answer simple questions about both colors and shapes using one or two words (e.g., "What shape is the orange?" "It's round."). Can use gestures to support responses about colors and shapes of food items (e.g., pointing to a round object when asked to find a shape).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage activates students' prior knowledge and introduces the target language in an engaging, low-stress manner, focusing on visual and kinesthetic learning for 1st graders.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up various realia or large flashcards of food items from previous lessons (e.g., an apple, a banana, a cracker, a mango) and asks "What is this?" and "What color is it?". Students respond chorally or individually with one-word answers (e.g., "Apple!", "Red!"). The teacher then introduces the concept of shapes using gestures, asking "What shape is the apple?" while tracing a circle in the air. This connects to the theme "It's a Square Cracker." by gradually bringing in shapes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target sentences clearly: "This is a red apple. It's round." and "This is a brown cracker. It's square." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /r/ in "round" and /k/ in "cracker" while showing the actual items. Students repeat chorally. The teacher also models gestures for "round" (circular hand movement) and "square" (making a square with fingers/hands). The teacher also introduces /æ/ in "apple" as part of phonemic awareness, saying "A-a-apple."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a square cracker) "Is this round or square?" (Expected answer: "Square!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a red apple) "Is this cracker red or brown?" (Expected answer: "Brown!")</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher reinforces correct answers and uses TPR for clarification (e.g., pointing to the shape/color as students respond).
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are exposed to new language (vocabulary, grammar) in a meaningful context related to "Shapes on Our Plates."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents a large, colorful poster or digital slideshow titled "Our Healthy Snack Plate" showing various fruits and a cracker, all clearly labeled with their shapes and colors. The focus is on new vocabulary like <i>pineapple</i>, <i>lemon</i>, <i>lime</i>, <i>mango</i>, <i>papaya</i>, alongside familiar items. Each item is shown with a sentence "This is a [color] [food]. It's [shape]." (e.g., "This is a yellow lemon. It's oval.") and "What shape is this [food]?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated picture chart/poster or digital slideshow focusing on individual food items (pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, orange, apple) with simple sentences. The register is informal, descriptive, and appropriate for young learners.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying food shapes: Identifying and describing foods by shape (e.g., "The cracker is square.") and Describing food: Using simple words to describe the colors and shapes of fruits and other foods.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher points to different foods on the poster and asks, "What food is this?" (e.g., "Mango!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Speaking/Pointing):</b> The teacher says a shape (e.g., "Find the round food!") and students point to all round foods on the poster. Then, the teacher asks, "What color is the round food?" (e.g., "Red apple!" "Orange!"). This reinforces color and shape identification.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Choral response):</b> The teacher points to a square cracker and says, "The cracker is round. True or false?" Students respond, "False!" or "True!" (pointing to a round item).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on controlled and semi-controlled practice to build accuracy in speaking the target structures and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Speaking:</b> Students practice naming colors and shapes of food items using adjective + noun and answering WH-questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: Adjective + noun (e.g., "round apple"), WH-questions ("What shape is it?"), Present simple ("Oranges are round.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, food, round, square, oval, big, small, orange, black, brown, white.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pragmatic: Asking about shapes in food, Identifying food shapes.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Look, Point, and Say"</b> (Choral and Individual Repetition)
                        <br>The teacher uses large flashcards or realia of food items (e.g., a yellow lemon, a green lime, a brown cracker, a round orange, an oval banana). The teacher holds up an item and asks, "What shape is this lemon?" Students respond chorally: "Oval!" Then, "What color is the lemon?" Students respond: "Yellow!" The teacher then models the full sentence: "This is a yellow lemon. It's oval." Students repeat chorally. This continues with <i>pineapple, mango, papaya, banana, cracker, orange</i> focusing on shape and color.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Color and Shape Sentence Builder"</b> (Guided Response)
                        <br>The teacher provides sentence frames on the board or orally: "This is a ____ ____. It's ____." or "The ____ is ____." The teacher holds up a flashcard (e.g., a green lime). Students complete the sentences, first chorally, then individually. "This is a green lime. It's oval." (Students practice `adjective + noun`). The teacher encourages the use of `demonstratives` "This is..." and "That is..." depending on proximity.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Shape & Color Question Swap"</b> (Peer Practice)
                        <br>Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small set of mixed food flashcards (e.g., apple, cracker, banana, juice box, pineapple, lemon). Partner A picks a card (without showing it to Partner B) and asks, "What shape is it?" Partner B guesses, "Is it round?" Partner A answers, "Yes, it is!" or "No, it isn't." Then Partner B asks, "What color is it?" Partner A answers, "It's red." Once guessed, they switch roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation, especially for /r/ and /k/.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language freely in a realistic, communicative task, focusing on fluency and interaction related to "Shapes on Our Plates."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Food Shape Detective"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a "Mystery Food Bag" containing various plastic food items or laminated picture cards (e.g., a round orange, a square cracker, an oval banana, a triangular slice of papaya, a yellow lemon, a red apple, a brown juice box). One student is the "Food Detective." This student reaches into the bag, picks a food item without looking at it, and describes it to their group members using color and shape. The other students ask questions to guess the food.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 1 (Detective) holds a hidden item and says, "I have a mystery food!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other students ask, "What shape is it?" (Using WH-questions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Detective responds, "It's round!" or "It's square!" (Using simple words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other students ask, "What color is it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Detective responds, "It's yellow!" (Naming primary/secondary colors).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other students guess, "Is it a lemon?" or "Is it a banana?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Detective confirms, "Yes, it is a lemon!" or "No, it isn't."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The role of the detective rotates so all students have a chance to describe and guess.</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher encourages students to use `gestures` to support their responses about colors and shapes (e.g., making a circle with their hands for "round"). The activity connects directly to "It's a Square Cracker." and "Shapes on Our Plates" by focusing on describing real-life food items. The focus is on `fluency`, `collaborative interaction`, and successful `communicative task completion` – identifying the mystery food.
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage involves formative assessment to check learning and allows students to reflect on their performance.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Food Shape Detective" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students continue their "Food Shape Detective" game. The teacher moves around, listening to groups and informally assessing individual student's speaking ability using a checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> (e.g., /r/ in "round," /k/ in "cracker," /æ/ in "apple").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., "round," "square," "oval," "red," "yellow," "cracker," "banana," "lemon").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Accuracy:</b> Can name primary/secondary colors and shapes. Can answer "What shape is it?" with one-word or simple sentences ("It's round," "A red apple").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates actively in group interaction, asks and answers questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of gestures:</b> (e.g., pointing to an object, drawing a shape in the air).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me (using gestures) a 'round' food you talked about today. What color is it?" (Students show and say the color.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you tell your friend a new food and its shape?" (Students briefly share with a peer).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages both student and teacher to reflect on the learning process and plan for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite food to describe today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new color or shape word did you learn today?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., Were students engaged in the "Food Shape Detective" game? Did they use target vocabulary and grammar to describe food items?)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students struggling to name shapes/colors or ask/answer simple questions? (e.g., More individual modeling, picture prompts, sentence starters).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about the shapes and colors of food. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build upon this by having students write simple sentences describing the colors and shapes of food items, perhaps by labeling drawings or completing sentences.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>